            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Father of Information Theory</strong><br>\nShannon is best known for founding information theory with a landmark paper in 1948. In this paper, he created a framework for understanding the transmission of information and the limits of signal processing. He introduced key concepts like \"bit\" and \"entropy,\" which became fundamental to the digital era. Shannon's information theory has applications in telecommunications, computer science, and many other fields.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Technology and Computing</strong><br>\nShannon's contributions significantly impacted technology, particularly in the development of the digital computer. His theories enabled more efficient data compression and error correction, leading to the development of the Internet and cellular technology. Shannon's work provided a way to measure information and paved the way for innovations in digital communication.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hobbies and Inventions</strong><br>\nShannon had a range of hobbies that included juggling, unicycling, and chess. He often combined his playful nature with his professional work, inventing devices like a juggling robot and a mechanical mouse that could navigate mazes. His home was filled with gadgets and machines, reflecting his endless creativity and sense of fun.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
