            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Years and Recognition</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Shannon received numerous awards and honors, including the National Medal of Science. Despite his achievements, he remained modest and focused on his work and hobbies. In his later years, Shannon suffered from Alzheimer's disease and lived a private life until his death in 2001.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shannon's Legacy in Education</strong><br>\nClaude Shannon's legacy extends to education, where his theories are taught in computer science, engineering, and mathematics courses worldwide. He is a role model for students, exemplifying how theoretical work can lead to practical applications. Teachers and textbooks often cite Shannon as a prime example of creativity in science and technology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Remembering Claude Shannon</strong><br>\nClaude Shannon's death in 2001 marked the end of a prolific era of innovation in communication and information. He is remembered not only for his scientific contributions but also for the curiosity and innovative spirit that drove his work. Shannon's legacy continues to influence modern technology and the ways we communicate, store, and process information.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
